1
00:00:01,399 --> 00:00:05,360
1984 by George Orwell

2
00:00:06,179 --> 00:00:09,359
PART 1 chapter 1.

3
00:00:09,359 --> 00:00:14,160
It was a bright cold day in April, and the clocks were striking 13.

4
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:18,480
Winston Smith, his gin nuzzled into his breast in an effort to escape the vile

5
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:23,100
wind, slipped quickly through the glass doors of victory Mansions, though not

6
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:27,359
quickly enough to prevent a swirl of gritty dust from entering along with him.

7
00:00:27,359 --> 00:00:31,079
The hallway smelt of boiled cabbage and old rag mats.

8
00:00:31,079 --> 00:00:35,399
At one end of it a colored poster, too large for indoor display, had been tacked

9
00:00:35,399 --> 00:00:36,539
to the wall.

10
00:00:36,539 --> 00:00:41,340
It depicted simply an enormous face, more than a meter wide: the face of a man

11
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:46,200
about 45 with a heavy black mustache and ruggedly handsome peaches

12
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:51,119
Winston made for the stairs. It was no use trying the lift. Even at the best of

13
00:00:51,119 --> 00:00:54,719
times it was seldom working, and had present the electric current was cut off

14
00:00:54,719 --> 00:00:56,878
during daylight hours.

15
00:00:56,878 --> 00:01:00,719
It was part of the economy drive in preparation for Hate Week.

16
00:01:00,719 --> 00:01:05,159
The flat was seven flights up, and Winston, who was 39 and had a varicose

17
00:01:05,159 --> 00:01:10,438
ulcer above his right ankle, went slowly, resting several times on the way.

18
00:01:10,438 --> 00:01:16,379
On each landing, opposite the lift shaft, the poster with the enormous face gazed from the wall.

19
00:01:16,379 --> 00:01:19,978
It was one of those pictures which is so contrived that the eyes follow you about

20
00:01:19,978 --> 00:01:21,719
when you move.

21
00:01:21,719 --> 00:01:26,340
BIG BROTHER IS WATCHING YOU, the caption beneath it ran.

22
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:29,938
Inside the flatter fruity voice was reading out a list of figures which had

23
00:01:29,938 --> 00:01:34,500
something to do with the production of pigahan The voice came from an oblong metal

24
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:39,420
plaque like a dulled mirror which formed part of the surface of the right-hand wall.

25
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:43,618
Winston turned a switch and the voice sank somewhat, though the words were

26
00:01:43,618 --> 00:01:44,879
still distinguishable.

27
00:01:44,879 --> 00:01:49,140
The instrument (the telescreen, it was called) could be dimmed, but there was no

28
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:51,659
way of shutting it off completely.

29
00:01:51,659 --> 00:01:56,519
He moved over to the window: a smallish, frail figure, the meaganess of

30
00:01:56,519 --> 00:02:01,560
his body merely emphasized by the blue overalls which were the uniform of the Party.

31
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:06,118
His hair was very fair, his face naturally sanguine, his skin roughened by

32
00:02:06,118 --> 00:02:11,699
coarse soap and blunt razor blades and the cold of the winter that had just ended.

33
00:02:11,699 --> 00:02:16,199
Outside, even through the shut window pane the world looked cold.

34
00:02:16,199 --> 00:02:20,159
Down in the street little eddies of wind were whirling dust and torn paper into

35
00:02:20,159 --> 00:02:25,139
spirals, and though the sun was shining into the sky a harsh blue, there seemed

36
00:02:25,139 --> 00:02:30,360
to be no color in anything, except the posters that were plastered everywhere.

37
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:35,400
The black mustachioed face gazed down from every commanding corner. There was

38
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:37,919
one on the house front immediately opposite.

39
00:02:37,919 --> 00:02:42,239
BIG BROTHER IS WATCHING YOU, the caption said, while the dark eyes looked deep

40
00:02:42,239 --> 00:02:44,039
into Winston's own.

41
00:02:44,039 --> 00:02:48,840
Down at street level another poster, torn at one corner, flapped fitfully in the

42
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:53,360
wind, alternately covering and uncovering the single word inksock

43
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:58,560
In the far distance a helicopter skimmed down between the roofs, hovered for an

44
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:02,939
instant like a blue bottle and darted away again with a curving flight.

45
00:03:02,939 --> 00:03:07,378
It was the police patrol, snooping into people's windows. The patrols did not

46
00:03:07,378 --> 00:03:12,139
matter, however. Only the Thought Police mattered.

47
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:15,719
Behind Winston's back the voice from the Telly screen was still babbling away

48
00:03:15,719 --> 00:03:20,340
about pig iron and the over fulfillment of the 9th Three-Year Plan.

49
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:24,840
The telescreen received and transmitted simultaneously and it sounds that

50
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:29,039
Winston made, above the level of a very low whisper, would be picked up by it;

51
00:03:29,039 --> 00:03:33,419
moreover, so long as he remained within the field of vision which the metal

52
00:03:33,419 --> 00:03:37,259
plaque commanded, he could be seen as well as heard.

53
00:03:37,259 --> 00:03:41,639
There was of course no way of knowing whether you were being watched at any given moment.

54
00:03:41,639 --> 00:03:45,840
How often, or on what system, the Thought Police plugged in on any individual wire

55
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,519
was guesswork.

56
00:03:47,519 --> 00:03:51,360
It was even conceivable that they watched everybody all the time.

57
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:56,098
But at any rate they could plug in your wire whenever they wanted to. You had to

58
00:03:56,098 --> 00:04:00,299
live— —did live, from habitat that became instinct— —in the assumption that every

59
00:04:00,299 --> 00:04:06,780
sound you made was overheard, and, except in darkness, every movement scrutinized

60
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:11,818
Winston kept his back turned to the telescreen. It was safer; though, as he

61
00:04:11,818 --> 00:04:14,580
well knew, even a back can be revealing.

62
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:19,319
A kilometer away the Ministry of Truth, his place of work, towered vast and white

63
00:04:19,319 --> 00:04:21,478
above the grimy landscape.

64
00:04:21,478 --> 00:04:26,819
This, he thought was a sort of vague distaste— —this was London, chief city of

65
00:04:26,819 --> 00:04:32,120
Airstrip 1 itself the third most populous of the provinces of Oceania.

66
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:36,000
He tried to squeeze out some childhood memory that should tell him whether

67
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
London had always been quite like this.

68
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,620
Were there always these vistas of rotting 19th century houses, their sides

69
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:47,819
shored up with the bulks of timber, their windows patched with cardboard and their

70
00:04:47,819 --> 00:04:53,100
roofs with corrugated iron, their crazy garden walls sagging in all directions?

71
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:57,180
And the bombed cites where the plaster dust swirled in the air and the Willow

72
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:01,620
herbs straggled over the heaps of rubble; and the places where the bombs had

73
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:05,699
cleared a larger patch and there had sprung up sordid colonies of wooden

74
00:05:05,699 --> 00:05:10,379
dwellings like chicken houses But it was no use, he could not remember:

75
00:05:10,379 --> 00:05:14,879
nothing remained of his childhood except a series of bright-lit tableaus

76
00:05:14,879 --> 00:05:19,379
occurring against no background and mostly unintelligible.

77
00:05:19,379 --> 00:05:24,180
The Ministry of Truth mini true in Newspeak Newspeak was the official

78
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:29,220
language of Oceania for an account of its structure and etymology C appendix

79
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:32,759
was startlingly different from any other object in sight.

80
00:05:32,759 --> 00:05:37,379
It was an enormous pyramidal structure of glittering white concrete, soaring up,

81
00:05:37,379 --> 00:05:41,339
terrace after terrace 300 meters into the air.

82
00:05:41,339 --> 00:05:45,538
From where Winston stood it was just possible to read, picked out on its white

83
00:05:45,538 --> 00:05:52,220
face in elegant lettering, the three slogans of the Party: WAR IS PEACE FREEDOM IS SLAVERY

84
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:55,560
IGNORANCE IS STRENGTH.

85
00:05:55,560 --> 00:05:59,220
The Ministry of Truth contained, it was said, three thousand rooms above ground

86
00:05:59,220 --> 00:06:03,000
level, and corresponding ramifications below.

87
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,740
Scattered about London there were just three other buildings of similar appearance and size.

88
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:11,220
So completely did they dwarf the surrounding architecture that from the

89
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:15,538
roof of Victory Mansions you could see all four of them simultaneously.

90
00:06:15,538 --> 00:06:18,720
They were the homes of the four Ministries between which the entire

91
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:21,600
apparatus of government was divided.

92
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:25,978
The Ministry of Truth, which concerned itself with news, entertainment, education

93
00:06:25,978 --> 00:06:27,538
and the fine arts.

94
00:06:27,538 --> 00:06:30,720
The Ministry of Peace, which concerned itself with war.

95
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:35,220
The Ministry of Love, which maintained law and order. And the Ministry of Plenty,

96
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:38,279
which was responsible for economic affairs.

97
00:06:38,279 --> 00:06:45,418
Their names, in Newspeak mini true mini packs mini love and Minnie plenty

98
00:06:45,418 --> 00:06:48,360
The Ministry of Love was the really frightening one.

99
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:50,699
There were no windows in it at all.

100
00:06:50,699 --> 00:06:55,560
Winston had never been inside the Ministry of Love, nor within half a kilometer of it.

101
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:59,100
It was a place impossible to enter except on official business, and then

102
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:03,600
only by penetrating through a maze of barbed wire entanglements, steel doors

103
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:06,300
and hidden machine gun nests.

104
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:10,079
Even the streets leading up to its outer barriers were roamed by guerrilla-faced

105
00:07:10,079 --> 00:07:15,180
guards in black uniforms, armed with jointed truncheons.

106
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:19,680
Winston turned around abruptly. He had set his features into the expression of

107
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:24,240
quiet optimism which it was advisable to wear when facing the telescreen.

108
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:26,939
He crossed the room into the tiny kitchen.

109
00:07:26,939 --> 00:07:30,598
By leaving the Ministry at this time of day he had sacrificed his lunch in the

110
00:07:30,598 --> 00:07:34,319
canteen, and he was aware that there was no food in the kitchen except a hunk of

111
00:07:34,319 --> 00:07:38,639
dark colored bread which had got to be saved for tomorrow's breakfast.

112
00:07:38,639 --> 00:07:42,240
He took down from the shelf a bottle of colorless liquid with a plain white

113
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:44,639
label marked VICTORY GIN.

114
00:07:44,639 --> 00:07:49,019
It gave off a sickly, oily smell, as of Chinese rice spirit

115
00:07:49,019 --> 00:07:53,759
Winston poured out nearly a teacup full nerfed himself for a shock, and gulped it

116
00:07:53,759 --> 00:07:56,639
down like a dose of medicine.

117
00:07:56,639 --> 00:08:00,720
Instantly his face turned scarlet and the water ran out of his eyes.

118
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:05,160
The stuff was like nitric acid, and moreover, in swallowing it one had the

119
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:08,639
sensation of being hit on the back of the head with a rubber club.

120
00:08:08,639 --> 00:08:12,660
The next moment, however, the burning in his belly died down and the world began

121
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:14,399
to look more cheerful.

122
00:08:14,399 --> 00:08:18,538
He took a cigarette from a crumpled packet marked VICTORY CIGARETTES and in

123
00:08:18,538 --> 00:08:23,160
cautiously held it upright, whereupon the tobacco fell out onto the floor.

124
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:27,180
With the next he was more successful. He went back to the living room and sat

125
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:31,019
down at a small table that stood it to the left of the telescreen.

126
00:08:31,019 --> 00:08:35,158
From the table drawer he took out a pen holder a bottle of ink and a thick

127
00:08:35,158 --> 00:08:40,139
quarter-sized black book with a red back and a marbled cover.

128
00:08:40,139 --> 00:08:44,038
For some reason the telescreen in the living room was in an unusual position.

129
00:08:44,038 --> 00:08:48,179
Instead of being placed, as was normal, in the end wall, where it could command the

130
00:08:48,179 --> 00:08:51,958
whole room, it was in the longer wall, opposite the window.

131
00:08:51,958 --> 00:08:56,220
To one side of it there was a shallow alcove in which Winston was now sitting,

132
00:08:56,220 --> 00:09:01,139
and which, when the flats were built, had probably been intended to hold bookshelves.

133
00:09:01,139 --> 00:09:05,339
By sitting in the alcove, and keeping well back, Winston was able to remain

134
00:09:05,339 --> 00:09:08,700
outside the range of the telescreen, so far as sight went.

135
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:12,480
He could be heard, of course, but so long as he stayed in his present position he

136
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:14,159
could not be seen.

137
00:09:14,159 --> 00:09:18,000
It was partly the unusual geography of the room that had suggested to him the

138
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,539
thing that he was now about to do.

139
00:09:21,539 --> 00:09:25,679
But it had also been suggested by the book that he had just taken out of the draw

140
00:09:25,679 --> 00:09:30,179
It was a peculiarly beautiful book it's smooth creamy paper, a little

141
00:09:30,179 --> 00:09:35,759
yellowed by age, was of a kind that had not been manufactured for at least 40 years past.

142
00:09:35,759 --> 00:09:39,360
He could guess, however, that the book was much older than that.

143
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:43,139
He had seen it lying in the window of a frousy little junk shop in a slummy

144
00:09:43,139 --> 00:09:46,980
quarter of the town (just what quarter he did not now remember)

145
00:09:46,980 --> 00:09:51,480
and had been stricken immediately by an overwhelming desire to possess it.

146
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:55,559
Party members were supposed not to go into ordinary shops dealing on the free

147
00:09:55,559 --> 00:09:59,700
market', it was called), but the rule was not strictly kept, because there were

148
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:03,480
various things such as shoelaces and razor blades which it was impossible to

149
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:05,639
get hold of in any other way.

150
00:10:05,639 --> 00:10:09,720
He had given a quick glance up and down the street and then had slipped inside

151
00:10:09,720 --> 00:10:14,580
and bought the book for 2.50 At the time he was not conscious of

152
00:10:14,580 --> 00:10:17,039
wanting it for any particular purpose.

153
00:10:17,039 --> 00:10:20,039
He had carried it guiltily home in his briefcase.

154
00:10:20,039 --> 00:10:25,019
Even with nothing written in it, it was a compromising possession.

155
00:10:25,019 --> 00:10:28,559
The thing that he was about to do was to open a diary.

156
00:10:28,559 --> 00:10:32,820
This was not illegal (nothing was illegal, since there were no longer any laws), but

157
00:10:32,820 --> 00:10:37,019
if detected it was reasonably certain that it would be punished by death, or at

158
00:10:37,019 --> 00:10:41,339
least by 25 years in a forced labor camp.

159
00:10:41,339 --> 00:10:45,899
Winston fitted a nib into the pen holder and sucked it to get the grease off.

160
00:10:45,899 --> 00:10:50,399
The pin was an archaic instrument, seldom used even for signatures, and he had

161
00:10:50,399 --> 00:10:54,360
procured one, furtively and with some difficulty, simply because of a feeling

162
00:10:54,360 --> 00:10:58,379
that the beautiful creamy paper deserved to be written on with a real nib instead

163
00:10:58,379 --> 00:11:03,778
of being scratched with an ink pencil Actually he was not used to writing by hand.

164
00:11:03,778 --> 00:11:07,919
Apart from very short notes, it was usual to dictate everything into the speak

165
00:11:07,919 --> 00:11:12,120
right which was of course impossible for his present purpose.

166
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:16,259
He dipped the pen into the ink and then faulted for just a second.

167
00:11:16,259 --> 00:11:18,899
A tremor had gone through his bowels.

168
00:11:18,899 --> 00:11:21,839
To mark the paper was the decisive act.

169
00:11:21,839 --> 00:11:28,620
In the small clumsy letters he wrote: April 4th, 1984..

170
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:30,059
He sat back.

171
00:11:30,059 --> 00:11:34,320
A sense of complete helplessness had descended upon him. To begin with he did

172
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:37,559
not know with any certainty that this was 1984..

173
00:11:37,559 --> 00:11:41,759
It must be round about that date, since he was fairly sure that his age was 39

174
00:11:41,759 --> 00:11:46,620
and he believed that he had been born in 1944 or 1945;.

175
00:11:46,620 --> 00:11:52,500
but it was never possible nowadays to pin down any date within a year or two.

176
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:56,940
For whom, it suddenly occurred to him to wonder, was he writing this diary?

177
00:11:56,940 --> 00:11:59,278
For the future, for the unborn.

178
00:11:59,278 --> 00:12:03,419
His mind hovered for a moment for under the doubtful date on the page, and then

179
00:12:03,419 --> 00:12:07,679
fetched up with a bump against the new speak word double think

180
00:12:07,679 --> 00:12:11,940
For the first time the magnitude of what he had undertaken came home to him.

181
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:14,458
How could you communicate with the future?

182
00:12:14,458 --> 00:12:18,720
It was of its nature impossible. Either the future would resemble the present, in

183
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:22,320
which case it would not listen to him: or it would be different from it, and his

184
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:25,578
predicament would be meaningless.

185
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:28,919
For some time he sat gazing stupidly at the paper.

186
00:12:28,919 --> 00:12:32,399
The telescreen had changed over to strident military music.

187
00:12:32,399 --> 00:12:37,019
It was curious that he seemed not merely to have lost the power of expressing himself,

188
00:12:37,019 --> 00:12:41,700
but even to have forgotten what it was that he had originally intended to say.

189
00:12:41,700 --> 00:12:45,839
For weeks past he had been making ready for this moment, and it had never crossed

190
00:12:45,839 --> 00:12:48,839
his mind that anything would be needed except courage.

191
00:12:48,839 --> 00:12:53,458
The actual writing would be easy. All he had to do was to transfer to paper the

192
00:12:53,458 --> 00:12:59,100
interminable restless monologue that had been running inside his head, literally for years.

193
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:03,360
At this moment, however, even the monologue had dried up. Moreover his

194
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:06,059
varicose ulcer had begun itching unbearably.

195
00:13:06,059 --> 00:13:10,379
He dared not scratch it, because if he did so it always became inflamed.

196
00:13:10,379 --> 00:13:13,919
The seconds were ticking by. He was conscious of nothing except the

197
00:13:13,919 --> 00:13:17,700
blankness of the page in front of him, the itching of the skin above his ankle,

198
00:13:17,700 --> 00:13:23,578
the blaring of the music, and a slight booziness caused by the Jinn

199
00:13:23,578 --> 00:13:29,458
Suddenly he began writing in sheer panic, only imperfectly aware of what he was sitting down.

200
00:13:29,458 --> 00:13:33,600
His small but childish handwriting straggled up and down the page, shedding

201
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:38,278
first its capital letters and finally even its full stops:

202
00:13:38,278 --> 00:13:42,299
April 4th, 1984.. Last night to the flicks.

203
00:13:42,299 --> 00:13:47,159
All war films. One very good one of a ship full of refugees being bombed

204
00:13:47,159 --> 00:13:49,078
somewhere in the Mediterranean.

205
00:13:49,078 --> 00:13:53,278
Audience much amused by shots of a great huge fat man trying to swim away with a

206
00:13:53,278 --> 00:13:57,360
helicopter after him. first you saw him wallowing along in the water like a

207
00:13:57,360 --> 00:14:01,320
porpoise, then you saw him through the helicopter's gun sights then he was full

208
00:14:01,320 --> 00:14:04,740
of holes and the sea around him turned pink and he sank as suddenly as though

209
00:14:04,740 --> 00:14:09,419
the holes had let in the water. audience shouting with laughter when he sank. then

210
00:14:09,419 --> 00:14:13,500
you saw a lifeboat full of children with a helicopter hovering over it.

211
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:17,039
there was a middle-aged woman might have been a duess sitting up in the bow with

212
00:14:17,039 --> 00:14:20,639
a little boy about three years old in her arms. little boy screaming with

213
00:14:20,639 --> 00:14:24,299
fright and hiding his head between her breasts as if he was trying to burrow

214
00:14:24,299 --> 00:14:27,899
right into her and the woman putting her arms around him and comforting him

215
00:14:27,899 --> 00:14:32,278
although she was blue with fright herself, all the time covering him up as much as

216
00:14:32,278 --> 00:14:35,578
possible as if she thought her arms could keep the bullets off him.

217
00:14:35,578 --> 00:14:40,559
then the helicopter planted a 20 kilo bomb in among them terrific flash and

218
00:14:40,559 --> 00:14:44,278
the boat went all to matchwood. then there was a wonderful shot of a child's

219
00:14:44,278 --> 00:14:48,299
arm going up up right up into the air a helicopter with a camera in its nose

220
00:14:48,299 --> 00:14:52,740
must have followed it up and there was a lot of applause from the party seats but

221
00:14:52,740 --> 00:14:56,220
a woman down in the parole part of the house suddenly started kicking up a fuss

222
00:14:56,220 --> 00:14:59,698
and shouting they didn't order of showed it not in front of the kids they didn't

223
00:14:59,698 --> 00:15:03,839
it ain't right not in front of kids it ain't until the police turned her turned

224
00:15:03,839 --> 00:15:07,139
her out i don't suppose anything happened to her nobody cares what the

225
00:15:07,139 --> 00:15:13,379
pros say typical Pro reaction they never— Winston stopped writing, partly because

226
00:15:13,379 --> 00:15:17,698
he was suffering from cramp. He did not know what had made him pour out this

227
00:15:17,698 --> 00:15:19,019
stream of rubbish.

228
00:15:19,019 --> 00:15:22,559
But the curious thing was that while he was doing so a totally different memory

229
00:15:22,559 --> 00:15:28,259
had clarified itself in his mind, to the point where he almost felt equal to writing it down.

230
00:15:28,259 --> 00:15:32,458
It was, he now realized because of this other incident that he had suddenly

231
00:15:32,458 --> 00:15:36,539
decided to come home and to begin the diary today.

232
00:15:36,539 --> 00:15:42,059
It had happened that morning at the Ministry, if anything so nebulous could be said to happen.

233
00:15:42,059 --> 00:15:46,679
It was nearly 1100 and in the Records Department, where Winston worked, they

234
00:15:46,679 --> 00:15:49,740
were dragging the chairs out of the cubicles and grouping them in the center

235
00:15:49,740 --> 00:15:54,440
of the hall, opposite the big telescreen, in preparation for the Two Minutes Hate.

236
00:15:54,440 --> 00:15:58,860
Winston was just taking his place in one of the middle rows when two people whom

237
00:15:58,860 --> 00:16:03,899
he knew by sight, but had never spoken to, came unexpectedly into the room.

238
00:16:03,899 --> 00:16:07,980
One of them was a girl whom he often passed in the corridors. He did not know

239
00:16:07,980 --> 00:16:11,399
her name, but he knew that she worked in the Fiction Department.

240
00:16:11,399 --> 00:16:15,360
Presumably— —since he had sometimes seen her with oily hands and carrying a

241
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:19,919
spanner— —she had some mechanical job on one of the novel writing machines.

242
00:16:19,919 --> 00:16:25,019
She was a bold looking girl, of about 27 with thick hair, a freckled face and

243
00:16:25,019 --> 00:16:27,059
swift, athletic movements.

244
00:16:27,059 --> 00:16:31,860
A narrow scarlet sash, emblem of the Junior Anti-Sex League, was wound several

245
00:16:31,860 --> 00:16:35,639
times around the waist of her overalls, just tightly enough to bring out the

246
00:16:35,639 --> 00:16:37,860
shapedness of her hips.

247
00:16:37,860 --> 00:16:41,578
Winston had disliked her from the very first moment of seeing her.

248
00:16:41,578 --> 00:16:45,600
He knew the reason. It was because of the atmosphere of hockey fields and cold

249
00:16:45,600 --> 00:16:50,159
baths and community hikes and general clean-mindedness which she managed to

250
00:16:50,159 --> 00:16:51,839
carry about with her.

251
00:16:51,839 --> 00:16:56,639
He disliked nearly all women, and especially the young and pretty ones. It

252
00:16:56,639 --> 00:17:00,419
was always the women, and above all the young ones, who were the most bigoted

253
00:17:00,419 --> 00:17:05,880
adherents of the Party, the swallowers of slogans, the amateur spies and noses out of

254
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:07,259
unorthodoxy.

255
00:17:07,259 --> 00:17:11,759
But this particular girl gave him the impression of being more dangerous than most.

256
00:17:11,759 --> 00:17:15,240
Once when they passed in the corridor she gave him a quick side long glance

257
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:20,338
which seemed to pierce right into him and for a moment had filled him with black terror.

258
00:17:20,338 --> 00:17:25,558
The idea had even crossed his mind that she might be an agent of the Thought Police.

259
00:17:25,558 --> 00:17:30,000
That, it was true, was very unlikely. Still, he continued to feel a peculiar

260
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:34,380
uneasiness, which had fear mixed up in it as well as hostility, whenever she was

261
00:17:34,380 --> 00:17:36,740
anywhere near him.

262
00:17:36,740 --> 00:17:41,339
The other person was a man named O'Brien, a member of the Inner Party and holder

263
00:17:41,339 --> 00:17:46,919
of some posts so important and remote that Winston had only a dim idea of its nature.

264
00:17:46,919 --> 00:17:51,119
A momentary hush passed over the group of people rounded the chairs as they saw

265
00:17:51,119 --> 00:17:54,119
the black overalls of an Inner Party member approaching.

266
00:17:54,119 --> 00:17:59,640
O'Brien was a large, burly man with a thick neck and a coarse, humorous, brutal face.

267
00:17:59,640 --> 00:18:04,140
In spite of his formidable appearance he had a certain charm of manner. He had a

268
00:18:04,140 --> 00:18:08,339
trick of resettling his spectacles on his nose which was curiously disarming—

269
00:18:08,339 --> 00:18:14,038
—in some indefinable way, curiously civilized It was a gesture of which, if anyone had

270
00:18:14,038 --> 00:18:19,859
still thought in such terms, might have recalled an 18th century nobleman offering his snuff box

271
00:18:19,859 --> 00:18:25,200
Winston had seen O'Brien perhaps a dozen times in almost as many years.

272
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:29,579
He felt deeply drawn to him, and not solely because he was intrigued by the

273
00:18:29,579 --> 00:18:34,440
contrast between O'Brien's urbane Manor and his prize Fighters physique.

274
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:38,700
Much more it was because of a secretly held belief— —or perhaps not even a belief,

275
00:18:38,700 --> 00:18:43,440
merely a hope— —that O'Brien's political orthodoxy was not perfect.

276
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:46,500
Something in his face suggested it irresistibly.

277
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:50,339
And again, perhaps it was not even unorthodoxy that was written in his face,

278
00:18:50,339 --> 00:18:52,740
but simply intelligence.

279
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:56,279
But at any rate he had the appearance of being a person that you could talk to, if

280
00:18:56,279 --> 00:19:00,298
somehow you could cheat the tele screen and get him alone. Winston had never made

281
00:19:00,298 --> 00:19:05,038
the smallest effort to verify this guess: indeed, there was no way of doing so.

282
00:19:05,038 --> 00:19:09,660
At this moment O'Brien glanced at his wristwatch, saw that it was nearly 1100

283
00:19:09,660 --> 00:19:15,058
and evidently decided to stay in the Records Department until the Two Minutes Hate was over.

284
00:19:15,058 --> 00:19:19,319
He took a chair in the same row as Winston, a couple of places away. A small,

285
00:19:19,319 --> 00:19:23,640
sandy-haired woman who worked in the next cubicle to Winston was between them.

286
00:19:23,640 --> 00:19:28,380
The girl with dark hair was sitting immediately behind.

287
00:19:28,380 --> 00:19:32,220
The next moment a hideous, grinding speech as of some monstrous machine

288
00:19:32,220 --> 00:19:36,599
running without oil, burst from the big telescreen at the end of the room.

289
00:19:36,599 --> 00:19:40,319
It was a noise that set one's teeth on edge and bristled the hair at the back

290
00:19:40,319 --> 00:19:41,579
of one's neck.

291
00:19:41,579 --> 00:19:44,339
The Hate had started.

292
00:19:44,339 --> 00:19:50,099
As usual, the face of Emmanuel Goldstein, the Enemy of the People, had flashed onto the screen.

293
00:19:50,099 --> 00:19:53,099
There were hisses here and there among the audience.

294
00:19:53,099 --> 00:19:57,660
The little sandy-haired woman gave a squeak of mingled fear and a disgust.

295
00:19:57,660 --> 00:20:01,980
Goldstein was the renegade and backslider who once, long ago (how long

296
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:05,640
ago, nobody quite remembered), had been one of the leading figures of the Party,

297
00:20:05,640 --> 00:20:09,859
almost on a level with Big Brother himself, and then had engaged in

298
00:20:09,859 --> 00:20:13,679
counter-revolutionary activities, had been condemned to death and had

299
00:20:13,679 --> 00:20:16,440
mysteriously escaped and disappeared.

300
00:20:16,440 --> 00:20:20,220
The programs of the Two Minutes Hate varied from day to day, but there was

301
00:20:20,220 --> 00:20:23,460
none in which Goldstein was not the principal figure.

302
00:20:23,460 --> 00:20:27,960
He was the primal traitor, the earliest defiler of the Party's purity.

303
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:32,819
All subsequent crimes against the Party, all treacheries, acts of sabotage,

304
00:20:32,819 --> 00:20:37,440
heresies, deviations, sprang directly out of his teaching.

305
00:20:37,440 --> 00:20:42,538
Somewhere or other he was still alive and hatching his conspiracies

306
00:20:42,538 --> 00:20:45,900
somewhere beyond the sea, under the protection of his foreign paymasters,

307
00:20:45,900 --> 00:20:52,940
perhaps even— —so it was occasionally rumored in some hiding place in Oceania itself.

308
00:20:52,940 --> 00:20:55,859
Winston's diaphragm was constricted.

309
00:20:55,859 --> 00:21:00,359
He could never see the face of Goldstein without a painful mixture of emotions.

310
00:21:00,359 --> 00:21:04,859
It was a lean Jewish face, with a great fuzzy Oriole of white hair and a small

311
00:21:04,859 --> 00:21:09,599
goatee beard— —a clever face, and yet somehow inherently despicable, with a

312
00:21:09,599 --> 00:21:13,500
kind of senile silliness in the long thin nose near the end of which a pair

313
00:21:13,500 --> 00:21:15,720
of spectacles was perched.

314
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:20,279
It resembled the face of a sheep, and the voice, too, had a sheep-like quality.

315
00:21:20,279 --> 00:21:24,900
Goldstein was delivering his usual venomous attack upon the doctrines of the Party—

316
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:28,679
—an attack so exaggerated and perverse that a child should have been able to

317
00:21:28,679 --> 00:21:32,640
see through it, and yet just plausible enough to fill one with an alarmed

318
00:21:32,640 --> 00:21:37,980
feeling that other people, less level-headed than oneself, might be taken in by it.

319
00:21:37,980 --> 00:21:41,759
He was abusing Big Brother, he was denouncing the dictatorship of the Party,

320
00:21:41,759 --> 00:21:45,960
he was demanding the immediate conclusion of peace with Eurasia, he was

321
00:21:45,960 --> 00:21:51,058
advocating freedom of speech, freedom of the press, freedom of assembly, freedom of

322
00:21:51,058 --> 00:21:55,259
thought, he was crying hysterically that the revolution had been betrayed— —and all

323
00:21:55,259 --> 00:21:59,099
this in rapid polysyllabic speech which was a sort of parody of the habitual

324
00:21:59,099 --> 00:22:03,359
style of the orators of the Party, and even contained new speak words:

325
00:22:03,359 --> 00:22:07,019
more news-speak words, indeed, than any Party member would normally use in real

326
00:22:07,019 --> 00:22:11,460
life. And all the while, lest one should be in any doubt as to the reality which

327
00:22:11,460 --> 00:22:15,720
Goldstein's specious claptrap covered, behind his head on the telescreen there

328
00:22:15,720 --> 00:22:19,980
marched the endless columns of the Eurasian army— row after row of

329
00:22:19,980 --> 00:22:24,480
solid-looking men with expressionless Asiatic faces, who swam up to the surface

330
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:28,859
of the screen and vanished, to be replaced by others exactly similar. The

331
00:22:28,859 --> 00:22:35,480
dull rhythmic of the Soldier's boots formed to the background to Goldstein's bleeding voice.

332
00:22:35,640 --> 00:22:40,259
Before the hated proceeded for 30 seconds, uncontrollable exclamations of

333
00:22:40,259 --> 00:22:43,259
rage were breaking out from half the people in the room.

334
00:22:43,259 --> 00:22:47,579
The self-satisfied Sheep like face on the screen, and the terrifying power of

335
00:22:47,579 --> 00:22:50,819
the Eurasian army behind it, were too much to be born

336
00:22:50,819 --> 00:22:56,099
besides, the sight or even the thought of Goldstein produced fear and anger automatically.

337
00:22:56,099 --> 00:23:00,839
He was an object of hatred more constant than either Eurasia or East Asia since

338
00:23:00,839 --> 00:23:05,579
when Oceania was at war with one of these powers it was generally at peace with the other.

339
00:23:05,579 --> 00:23:09,419
But what was strange was that although Goldstein was hated and despised by

340
00:23:09,419 --> 00:23:13,798
everybody, although every day at a thousand times a day, on platforms, on the

341
00:23:13,798 --> 00:23:18,900
telescreen, in newspapers, in books, his theories were refuted, smashed, ridiculed,

342
00:23:18,900 --> 00:23:23,519
held up to the general gaze for the pitiful rubbish that they were— —in spite

343
00:23:23,519 --> 00:23:26,579
of all this, his influence never seemed to grow less.

344
00:23:26,579 --> 00:23:30,419
Always there were fresh dupes waiting to be seduced by him.

345
00:23:30,419 --> 00:23:33,960
A day never passed when spies and saboteurs acting under his directions

346
00:23:33,960 --> 00:23:36,480
were not unmasked by the Thought Police.

347
00:23:36,480 --> 00:23:40,679
He was the commander of a vast shadowy army, an underground network of

348
00:23:40,679 --> 00:23:44,099
conspirators dedicated to the overthrow of the State.

349
00:23:44,099 --> 00:23:46,619
The Brotherhood, its name was supposed to be.

350
00:23:46,619 --> 00:23:50,940
There were also whispered stories of a terrible book, a compendium of all the

351
00:23:50,940 --> 00:23:56,339
heresies, of which Goldstein was the author and which circulated clandestinely here and there.

352
00:23:56,339 --> 00:24:01,319
It was a book without a title. People referred to it, if at all, simply as the

353
00:24:01,319 --> 00:24:05,940
book. But one knew of such things only through vague rumors Neither the

354
00:24:05,940 --> 00:24:09,659
Brotherhood nor the book was the subject that any ordinary Party member would

355
00:24:09,659 --> 00:24:12,599
mention if there was a way of avoiding it.

356
00:24:12,599 --> 00:24:17,279
In its second minute the Hate rose to a frenzy. People were leaping up and down

357
00:24:17,279 --> 00:24:20,460
in their places and shouting at the tops of their voices in an effort to drown

358
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:23,819
the maddening bleeding voice that came from the screen.

359
00:24:23,819 --> 00:24:27,659
The little sandy-haired woman had turned bright pink, and her mouth was opening

360
00:24:27,659 --> 00:24:30,058
and shutting like that of a landed fish.

361
00:24:30,058 --> 00:24:34,380
Even O'Brien's heavy face was flushed. He was sitting very straight in his chair,

362
00:24:34,380 --> 00:24:40,140
his powerful chest swelling and quivering as though he was standing up to the assault of a wave.

363
00:24:40,140 --> 00:24:46,558
The dark-haired girl behind Winston had begun crying out 'Swine! Swine! Swine!', and

364
00:24:46,558 --> 00:24:50,700
suddenly she picked up a heavy Newspeak dictionary and flung it at the screen.

365
00:24:50,700 --> 00:24:55,740
It struck Goldstein's nose and bounced off: the voice continued inexorably.

366
00:24:55,740 --> 00:25:00,119
In a lucid moment Winston found that he was shouting with the others and kicking

367
00:25:00,119 --> 00:25:03,419
his heel violently against the rung of his chair.

368
00:25:03,419 --> 00:25:07,798
The horrible thing about the Two Minutes Hate was not that one was obliged to act

369
00:25:07,798 --> 00:25:12,419
apart but on the contrary that it was impossible to avoid joining in.

370
00:25:12,419 --> 00:25:15,720
Within 30 seconds any pretense was always unnecessary.

371
00:25:15,720 --> 00:25:19,798
A hideous ecstasy of fear and vindictiveness, a desire to kill, to

372
00:25:19,798 --> 00:25:24,058
torture, to smash faces in with a sledgehammer, seemed to flow through the

373
00:25:24,058 --> 00:25:27,900
whole group of people like an electric current, turning one even against one's

374
00:25:27,900 --> 00:25:31,019
will into a grimacing, screaming lunatic.

375
00:25:31,019 --> 00:25:35,460
And yet the rage that one felt was an abstract, undirected emotion which could

376
00:25:35,460 --> 00:25:39,179
be switched from one object to another like the flame of a blow lamp

377
00:25:39,179 --> 00:25:44,038
Thus, at one moment Winston's hatred was not turned against Goldstein at all, but,

378
00:25:44,038 --> 00:25:48,480
on the contrary, against Big Brother, the Party and the Thought Police;

379
00:25:48,480 --> 00:25:52,319
and at such moments his heart went out to the lonely, derided heretic on the

380
00:25:52,319 --> 00:25:56,940
screen Soul guardian of truth and sanity in a world of lies.

381
00:25:56,940 --> 00:26:00,720
And yet the very next instant he was at one with the people about him, and all

382
00:26:00,720 --> 00:26:04,319
that was said of Goldstein seems to him to be true.

383
00:26:04,319 --> 00:26:08,640
At those moments his secret loathing of Big Brother changed into adoration, and

384
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:13,019
Big Brother seemed to tower up, an invincible, fearless protector, standing

385
00:26:13,019 --> 00:26:17,159
like a rock against the hordes of Asia, and Goldstein, in spite of his isolation,

386
00:26:17,159 --> 00:26:21,778
his helplessness and the doubts that hung about his very existence, seemed

387
00:26:21,778 --> 00:26:26,159
like some sinister enchanter, capable by the mere power of his voice of wrecking

388
00:26:26,159 --> 00:26:31,619
the structure of civilization It was even possible, at moments, to

389
00:26:31,619 --> 00:26:34,679
switch one's hatred this way or that by a voluntary act.

390
00:26:34,679 --> 00:26:38,278
Suddenly, by the sort of violent effort with which one wrenches one's head away

391
00:26:38,278 --> 00:26:42,419
from the pillow in a nightmare, Winston succeeded in transferring his hatred

392
00:26:42,419 --> 00:26:46,558
from the face on the screen to the dark-haired girl behind him.

393
00:26:46,558 --> 00:26:51,240
Vivid, beautiful hallucinations flashed through his mind. He would flog her to

394
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:54,960
death with a rubber truncheon. He would tie her naked to a stake and shoot her

395
00:26:54,960 --> 00:26:59,038
full of arrows like Saint Sebastian. He would ravish her and cut her throat at

396
00:26:59,038 --> 00:27:00,839
the moment of climax.

397
00:27:00,839 --> 00:27:05,819
Better than before, moreover, he realized why it was that he hated her. He hated

398
00:27:05,819 --> 00:27:10,019
her because she was young and pretty and sexless, because he wanted to go to bed

399
00:27:10,019 --> 00:27:14,159
with her and would never do so, because around her sweet supple waist, which

400
00:27:14,159 --> 00:27:17,880
seemed to ask you to encircle it with your arm, there was only the odious

401
00:27:17,880 --> 00:27:22,460
scarlet sash, aggressive symbol of chastity.

402
00:27:22,500 --> 00:27:26,880
The Hate rose to its climax. The voice of Goldstein had become an actual sheep's

403
00:27:26,880 --> 00:27:30,960
pleat and for an instant the face changed into that of a sheep.

404
00:27:30,960 --> 00:27:34,679
Then the Sheep face melted into the figure of a Eurasian soldier who seemed

405
00:27:34,679 --> 00:27:39,240
to be advancing, huge and terrible, his submachine gun roaring, and seeming to

406
00:27:39,240 --> 00:27:42,419
spring out of the surface of the screen, so that some of the people in the front

407
00:27:42,419 --> 00:27:45,599
row actually flinched backwards in their seats.

408
00:27:45,599 --> 00:27:49,319
But in the same moment, drawing a deep sigh of relief from everybody, the

409
00:27:49,319 --> 00:27:53,700
hostile figure melted into the face of Big Brother blackhead black mustache

410
00:27:53,700 --> 00:27:59,759
full of power and mysterious calm, and so vast that it almost filled up the screen.

411
00:27:59,759 --> 00:28:01,919
Nobody heard what Big Brother was saying.

412
00:28:01,919 --> 00:28:05,519
It was merely a few words of encouragement, the sort of words that are

413
00:28:05,519 --> 00:28:09,179
uttered in the din of battle, not distinguishable individually but

414
00:28:09,179 --> 00:28:12,659
restoring confidence by the fact of being spoken.

415
00:28:12,659 --> 00:28:16,679
Then the face of Big Brother faded away and instead of the three slogans of the

416
00:28:16,679 --> 00:28:25,200
Party stood out in bold's capitals: WAR IS PEACE FREEDOM IS SLAVERY IGNORANCE IS STRENGTH.

417
00:28:25,200 --> 00:28:28,558
But the face of Big Brother seemed to persist for several seconds on the

418
00:28:28,558 --> 00:28:32,159
screen, as though the impact that it had made on everyone's eyeballs was too

419
00:28:32,159 --> 00:28:34,440
vivid to wear off immediately.

420
00:28:34,440 --> 00:28:37,919
The little Sandy head woman had flung herself forward over the back of the

421
00:28:37,919 --> 00:28:42,839
chair in front of her. With a tremulous murmur that sounded like 'My savior she

422
00:28:42,839 --> 00:28:45,659
extended her arms towards the screen.

423
00:28:45,659 --> 00:28:51,900
Then she buried her face in her hands. It was apparent that she was uttering a prayer.

424
00:28:51,900 --> 00:28:59,900
At this moment the entire group of people broke into a deep, slow, rhythmical chant of bb

425
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:04,859
over and over again, very slowly, with a long pause between the first 'B' and the

426
00:29:04,859 --> 00:29:10,259
second— —a heavy, murmurous sound, somehow curiously savage, in the background of

427
00:29:10,259 --> 00:29:14,700
which one seemed to hear the stamp of naked feet and the throbbing of tomtoms

428
00:29:14,700 --> 00:29:19,500
For perhaps as much as 30 seconds they kept it up. It was a refrain that was

429
00:29:19,500 --> 00:29:23,819
often heard in moments of overwhelming emotion. Partly it was a sort of hymn to

430
00:29:23,819 --> 00:29:28,679
the wisdom and majesty of Big Brother, but still more it was an act of self-hypnosis

431
00:29:28,679 --> 00:29:32,880
a deliberate drowning of consciousness by means of rhythmic noise.

432
00:29:32,880 --> 00:29:35,940
Winston's entrails seemed to grow cold.

433
00:29:35,940 --> 00:29:39,898
In the Two Minutes Haiti could not help sharing in the general delirium, but this

434
00:29:39,898 --> 00:29:45,599
subhuman chanting of b b b b always filled him with horror.

435
00:29:45,599 --> 00:29:49,679
Of course he chartered with the rest: it was impossible to do otherwise.

436
00:29:49,679 --> 00:29:53,519
To dissemble your feelings, to control your face, to do what everyone else was

437
00:29:53,519 --> 00:29:56,099
doing, was an instinctive reaction.

438
00:29:56,099 --> 00:29:59,339
But there was a space of a couple of seconds during which the expression of

439
00:29:59,339 --> 00:30:02,460
his eyes might conceivably have betrayed him.

440
00:30:02,460 --> 00:30:06,599
And it was exactly at this moment that the significant thing happened— —if, indeed,

441
00:30:06,599 --> 00:30:09,000
it did happen.

442
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,700
Momentarily he caught O'Brien's eye.

443
00:30:11,700 --> 00:30:13,740
O'Brien had stood up.

444
00:30:13,740 --> 00:30:17,339
He had taken off his spectacles and was in the act of resettling them on his

445
00:30:17,339 --> 00:30:20,220
nose with his characteristic gesture.

446
00:30:20,220 --> 00:30:23,700
But there was a fraction of a second when their eyes met, and for as long as

447
00:30:23,700 --> 00:30:28,798
it took to happen Winston knew— —yes, he knew!— !—that O'Brien was thinking the same

448
00:30:28,798 --> 00:30:30,480
thing as himself.

449
00:30:30,480 --> 00:30:35,159
An unmistakable message had passed. It was as though their two minds had opened

450
00:30:35,159 --> 00:30:38,940
and the thoughts were flowing from one end to the other through their eyes.

451
00:30:38,940 --> 00:30:44,640
'I am with you,' O'Brien seemed to be saying to him. 'I know precisely what you are feeling.

452
00:30:44,640 --> 00:30:48,419
I know all about your contempt, your hatred you're disgust.

453
00:30:48,419 --> 00:30:51,960
But don't worry, I am on your side!'

454
00:30:51,960 --> 00:30:55,619
And then the Flesh of intelligence was gone, and O'Brien's face was as

455
00:30:55,619 --> 00:30:58,859
inscrutable as everybody else's.

456
00:30:58,859 --> 00:31:02,819
That was all, and he was already uncertain whether it had happened. Such

457
00:31:02,819 --> 00:31:07,319
incidents never had any sequel. All that they did was to keep alive in him the

458
00:31:07,319 --> 00:31:12,240
belief, or hope, that others beside himself were the enemies of the Party.

459
00:31:12,240 --> 00:31:16,440
Perhaps the rumors of vast underground to conspiracies were true after all—

460
00:31:16,440 --> 00:31:18,960
—perhaps the Brotherhood really existed!

461
00:31:18,960 --> 00:31:22,200
It was impossible, in spite of the endless arrests and confessions and

462
00:31:22,200 --> 00:31:26,278
executions, to be sure that the Brotherhood was not simply a myth.

463
00:31:26,278 --> 00:31:29,099
Some days he believed in it, some days not.

464
00:31:29,099 --> 00:31:32,519
There was no evidence, only fleeting glimpses that might mean anything or

465
00:31:32,519 --> 00:31:36,960
nothing: snatches of overheard conversation, faint scribbles on lavatory

466
00:31:36,960 --> 00:31:41,759
walls— —once, even, when two strangers met, a small movement of the hand which had

467
00:31:41,759 --> 00:31:44,880
looked as though it might be a signal of recognition.

468
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:48,960
It was all guesswork: very likely he had imagined everything.

469
00:31:48,960 --> 00:31:52,740
He had gone back to his cubicle without looking at O'Brien again.

470
00:31:52,740 --> 00:31:56,759
The idea of following up their momentary contact hardly crossed his mind.

471
00:31:56,759 --> 00:32:01,500
It would have been inconceivably dangerous even if he had known how to set about doing it.

472
00:32:01,500 --> 00:32:06,179
For a second, two seconds, they had exchanged an equivocal glance, and that

473
00:32:06,179 --> 00:32:07,558
was the end of the story.

474
00:32:07,558 --> 00:32:13,319
But even that was a memorable event in the locked loneliness in which one had to live.

475
00:32:13,319 --> 00:32:17,700
Winston roused himself and sat up straighter. He let out a belch. The gin

476
00:32:17,700 --> 00:32:19,859
was rising from his stomach.

477
00:32:19,859 --> 00:32:24,000
His eyes refocused on the page. He discovered that while he sat helplessly

478
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:27,839
musing he had also been writing, as though by automatic action.

479
00:32:27,839 --> 00:32:31,859
And it was no longer the same cramped awkward handwriting as before.

480
00:32:31,859 --> 00:32:37,919
His pen had slid voluptuously over the smooth paper, printing in large neat capitals—

481
00:32:37,919 --> 00:32:45,659
DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER

482
00:32:45,659 --> 00:32:50,519
DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER over and over again, filling half a page.

483
00:32:50,519 --> 00:32:53,099
He could not help feeling a twinge of panic.

484
00:32:53,099 --> 00:32:57,000
It was absurd, since the writing of those particular words was not more dangerous

485
00:32:57,000 --> 00:33:00,898
than the initial act of opening the diary; but for a moment he was tempted to

486
00:33:00,898 --> 00:33:05,460
tear out the spoiled pages and abandon the enterprise altogether.

487
00:33:05,460 --> 00:33:10,019
He did not do so, however, because he knew that it was useless. Whether he wrote

488
00:33:10,019 --> 00:33:13,980
DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER, or whether he refrained from writing it, made no difference.

489
00:33:13,980 --> 00:33:17,278
Whether he went on with the diary, or whether he did not go on with it, made no

490
00:33:17,278 --> 00:33:18,240
difference.

491
00:33:18,240 --> 00:33:22,500
The Thought Police would get him just the same. He had committed— —would still

492
00:33:22,500 --> 00:33:27,298
have committed, even if he had never set pen to paper— —the essential crime that contained all

493
00:33:27,298 --> 00:33:32,880
others in itself thought crime they called it thought crime was not a thing that could be

494
00:33:32,880 --> 00:33:36,419
concealed forever You might dodge successfully for a while,

495
00:33:36,419 --> 00:33:41,159
even for years, but sooner or later they were bound to get you.

496
00:33:41,159 --> 00:33:46,380
It was always at night— —the arrests invariably happened at night. The sudden

497
00:33:46,380 --> 00:33:50,398
jerk out of sleep, the rough hand shaking your shoulder, the lights glaring in your

498
00:33:50,398 --> 00:33:53,640
eyes, the ring of hard faces round to the bed.

499
00:33:53,640 --> 00:33:58,079
In the vast majority of cases there was no trial, no report of the arrest.

500
00:33:58,079 --> 00:34:02,038
People simply disappeared, always during the night.

501
00:34:02,038 --> 00:34:06,000
Your name was removed from the registers, every record of everything you had ever

502
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:10,378
done was wiped out, your one-time existence was denied and then forgotten.

503
00:34:10,378 --> 00:34:16,940
You were abolished, annihilated: vaporized was the usual word.

504
00:34:17,039 --> 00:34:21,300
For a moment he was seized by a kind of hysteria. He began writing in a hurried

505
00:34:21,300 --> 00:34:25,139
untidy scroll they'll shoot me i don't care they'll

506
00:34:25,139 --> 00:34:28,559
shoot me in the back of the neck i don't care down with big brother they always

507
00:34:28,559 --> 00:34:32,760
shoot you in the back of the neck i don't care down with big brother—

508
00:34:32,760 --> 00:34:37,679
He sat back in his chair, slightly ashamed of himself, and laid down the pen.

509
00:34:37,679 --> 00:34:39,659
The next moment he started violently.

510
00:34:39,659 --> 00:34:42,918
There was a knocking at the door.

511
00:34:42,918 --> 00:34:44,820
Already!

512
00:34:44,820 --> 00:34:48,780
He sat as still as a mouse, in the futile hope that whoever it was might go away

513
00:34:48,780 --> 00:34:50,878
after a single attempt.

514
00:34:50,878 --> 00:34:53,579
But no, the knocking was repeated.

515
00:34:53,579 --> 00:34:56,099
The worst thing of all would be to delay.

516
00:34:56,099 --> 00:35:01,320
His heart was thumping like a drum, but his face, from long habit, was probably expressionless.

517
00:35:01,320 --> 00:35:05,000
He got up and moved heavily towards the door
